T02.01|A percentage describes a fraction of a base. Percentage change divides the change by the original value; percentage points subtract two percentage levels. One basis point is 0.01 percentage point. Confusing these units can greatly exaggerate or understate interest-rate and margin changes.~~The denominator matters. A 50% fall followed by a 50% rise does not restore the starting value because the second change uses a smaller base.|Percentage: Part divided by whole, multiplied by 100.;;Percentage change: Change divided by starting value.;;Percentage point: Absolute difference between percentage levels.;;Basis point: One hundredth of a percentage point.|Revenue rises from ₹100 to ₹120 lakh; margin rises from 10% to 12%.~~Revenue growth = (120 − 100) / 100 = 20%.;;Margin improvement = 12% − 10% = 2 percentage points = 200 basis points.;;Relative margin growth = (12 − 10) / 10 = 20%.~~The margin rose 2 percentage points, not 2%.~~Both margin statements can be correct if their units are explicit; they answer different questions.|Identify the denominator.;;State the measurement unit.;;Use the original base for relative change.
T02.02|Ratios compare compatible quantities; weighted averages reflect how much each observation contributes. An equal-weight average is appropriate only when equal contribution is intended. Financial indices combine constituent values using a stated base and weighting rule.~~For portfolio returns, use beginning-period capital weights unless the methodology explicitly handles intra-period flows. Averaging company margins without revenue weights describes a typical company rather than the aggregate business.|Simple ratio: One compatible quantity divided by another.;;Equal-weight mean: Each observation receives the same weight.;;Weighted mean: Values multiplied by weights summing to one.;;Index: A relative measure normalised to a base.|Invest ₹80 lakh in an asset returning 10% and ₹20 in one returning −5%, with no flows.~~Weights are 80% and 20%.;;Portfolio return = 0.80 × 10% + 0.20 × (−5%) = 7%.;;Ending capital = ₹88 + ₹19 = ₹107.~~The 7% aggregate return differs from the 2.5% equal-weight mean.~~Weights must correspond to the quantity being aggregated; ending weights would mix performance with the initial allocation.|Check units and definitions.;;Confirm weights sum to one.;;Reconcile the average with aggregate totals.
T02.03|Simple interest applies the rate to original principal only. Compound interest also earns interest on accumulated interest. For principal P, rate r and n periods, simple future value is P(1 + rn), while compound future value is P(1 + r)^n.~~Rates and periods must match: a monthly rate needs a monthly period count. Compounding frequency matters when a quoted annual nominal rate is divided across subperiods.|Simple interest: Interest on original principal.;;Periodic compounding: Interest added at specified intervals.;;Continuous compounding: Limiting convention expressed as Pe^(rt).|Deposit ₹100 at 10% annually for three years, with no withdrawal.~~Simple value = ₹100 × (1 + 0.10 × 3) = ₹130.;;Compound value = ₹100 × 1.10³ = ₹133.10.;;Third-year compound interest is 10% of ₹121 = ₹12.10.~~Compounding produces ₹3.10 more than simple interest in this case.~~The difference grows with time and rate; actual product terms determine how interest is credited and available.|Read the compounding convention.;;Match rate and time units.;;Include deposits, withdrawals and fees separately.
T02.04|An arithmetic return is ending value divided by beginning value minus one, adjusted appropriately for distributions. A geometric average summarises compounded multi-period growth. A logarithmic return is ln(ending/beginning); log returns add across time, while ordinary returns multiply through growth factors.~~Arithmetic averages are useful for some one-period expectations but overstate realised compounded growth when returns vary. A geometric return requires positive growth factors and does not resolve external cash-flow timing.|Simple return: Percentage gain or loss for a period.;;Arithmetic average: Sum of periodic returns divided by count.;;Geometric average: Compound growth per period.;;Log return: Natural logarithm of the growth factor.|A ₹100 investment rises 20% then falls 20%, with no distributions.~~Ending value = ₹100 × 1.20 × 0.80 = ₹96.;;Arithmetic average = (20% − 20%) / 2 = 0%.;;Geometric annual return = √0.96 − 1 ≈ −2.02%.~~Zero average simple return did not prevent a 4% cumulative loss.~~Log returns sum to ln(0.96); exponentiating their sum recovers the cumulative growth factor.|Specify distribution treatment.;;Use multiplication for compounded simple returns.;;Distinguish averages from investor cash-flow returns.
T02.05|CAGR is the constant annual compound rate connecting an opening value to an ending value: (ending/beginning)^(1/years) − 1. It smooths the path and does not describe volatility or the sequence of intervening returns. Annualisation scales a period measure using an appropriate convention.~~Multiplying a short-period return by the number of periods is a simple annualisation, while compounding uses (1 + period return)^periods − 1. Neither convention proves the short-period result will recur.|CAGR: Endpoint compound growth over multiple years.;;Simple annualisation: Linear scaling of a period rate.;;Compound annualisation: Repetition through growth factors.|Value grows from ₹100 to ₹144 in two years. Separately assume a monthly return of 1%.~~CAGR = (144 / 100)^(1/2) − 1 = 20%.;;Simple annualisation of 1% monthly is 12%.;;Compound annualisation is 1.01¹² − 1 ≈ 12.68%.~~Compounding and linear scaling produce different annual figures.~~CAGR ignores interim flows; annualised short-period performance is a mathematical translation, not a forecast.|Count elapsed years accurately.;;State the annualisation method.;;Handle contributions and withdrawals separately.
T02.06|Exponents represent repeated multiplication, and logarithms reverse exponentiation. Growth equations use these tools to solve for a future amount, growth rate or time. If FV = PV(1 + r)^n, then n = ln(FV/PV) / ln(1 + r).~~These equations assume the growth rule is stable. Real revenue, prices and investment returns need not grow at one fixed rate. A mathematical solution should be checked against feasible business capacity and economic constraints.|Exponentiation: Build a growth factor over periods.;;Logarithm: Recover an exponent or transform multiplicative relationships.;;Growth equation: Solve for value, rate or time under stated assumptions.|How long does ₹100 take to double at a constant 10% annual compound rate?~~Set 200 = 100 × 1.10^n.;;Solve n = ln(2) / ln(1.10) ≈ 7.27 years.;;At whole-year crediting dates, value first exceeds ₹200 at year 8.~~The mathematical crossing time and actual product payment date can differ.~~The rule of 72 gives approximately 7.2 years here, a useful estimate rather than an exact identity.|Check logarithm domains.;;Use compatible time units.;;Distinguish continuous mathematical time from contractual dates.
T02.07|Probability quantifies likelihood; conditional probability updates likelihood when information is known. Bayes' theorem combines prior probabilities with how often a signal appears in each group. A highly accurate-looking signal can still produce many false positives when the event is rare.~~The base rate is essential in credit screening and fraud detection. Sensitivity measures detection among true events; specificity measures correct rejection among non-events. Neither alone equals the probability an alert is a genuine event.|Prior probability: Belief before observing the signal.;;Conditional probability: Likelihood given specified information.;;Posterior probability: Updated belief after the signal.|Among 1,000 accounts, 10 are fraudulent. A detector flags 90% of frauds and 10% of legitimate accounts.~~True alerts = 10 × 90% = 9.;;False alerts = 990 × 10% = 99.;;Fraud probability given an alert = 9 / (9 + 99) ≈ 8.33%.~~Most alerts are false positives despite 90% sensitivity.~~Changing the population's fraud prevalence changes the posterior even if detector characteristics remain unchanged.|Include base rates.;;Build a two-way outcome table.;;Match conditional probabilities to the question asked.
T02.08|Expected value is a probability-weighted average outcome. Variance measures squared deviations from that expectation; standard deviation returns dispersion to the original units. Covariance measures how two variables move together in their respective units and enters portfolio-risk calculations.~~An expected payoff need not be a possible payoff. Standard deviation treats positive and negative deviations alike and does not fully describe asymmetric or extreme loss risk.|Expectation: Sum of probability × outcome.;;Variance and standard deviation: Dispersion around the mean.;;Covariance: Joint co-movement in scale-dependent units.|A return is +10% or −10%, each with probability 50%.~~Expected return = 0.5 × 10% + 0.5 × (−10%) = 0%.;;Variance = 0.5 × 0.10² + 0.5 × (−0.10)² = 0.01.;;Standard deviation = √0.01 = 10%.~~Zero expected return coexists with substantial uncertainty.~~For two assets, portfolio variance also contains covariance; averaging their individual standard deviations generally gives the wrong risk.|Use probabilities summing to one.;;Keep percentages and decimals consistent.;;Assess tails and dependence alongside standard deviation.
T02.09|Correlation standardises covariance to a value between −1 and +1, measuring linear association. Positive values indicate co-movement, negative values opposing movement, and zero indicates no linear association. Zero correlation does not establish independence, and association does not prove causation.~~Financial correlations can change with regimes and crises. Two assets can seem unrelated in ordinary periods but suffer together during stress, weakening diversification when it is most needed.|Pearson correlation: Linear co-movement.;;Rank correlation: Association in ordered ranks.;;Conditional correlation: Co-movement within a particular regime or sample.|Two equally weighted assets each have 10% standard deviation. Compare correlation 1 and 0.~~At correlation 1, variance = 0.25 × 0.01 + 0.25 × 0.01 + 2 × 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.01 = 0.01.;;At correlation 0, variance = 0.005.;;Standard deviations are 10% and √0.005 ≈ 7.07%.~~Lower correlation reduces portfolio volatility in this model.~~Estimated historical correlation is not a guarantee of future diversification or protection against all losses.|Inspect the sample and regime.;;Distinguish linear association from causation.;;Stress-test dependence rather than relying on one estimate.
T02.10|A probability distribution describes possible outcomes and their likelihoods. Skewness concerns asymmetry; kurtosis describes aspects of tail weight and concentration relative to a reference distribution. Fat-tailed returns can produce extreme outcomes more often than a normal model suggests.~~The mean and standard deviation do not uniquely define risk. Two distributions can share those measures but differ greatly in downside probability, loss severity and exposure to rare events.|Discrete distribution: Separate possible outcomes.;;Continuous distribution: Probability over a continuum.;;Skewed or heavy-tailed distribution: Asymmetry or elevated extreme-outcome likelihood.|A strategy earns ₹1 lakh in 99 out of 100 equally likely outcomes and loses ₹50 in the remaining outcome.~~Expected result = 0.99 × ₹1 + 0.01 × (−₹50) = ₹0.49.;;The common result is a small gain, but the rare loss is much larger.;;Assess whether reserves and position size can survive that loss.~~A positive expectation can hide an adverse payoff shape.~~An observed run of gains does not rule out a large unobserved tail event; probabilities themselves may also be estimated poorly.|Inspect the full payoff distribution.;;Separate frequency from severity.;;Test extreme outcomes and parameter uncertainty.
T02.11|Sampling uses part of a population to estimate its properties. A confidence interval is a procedure that, under its assumptions, covers the true parameter at the stated long-run rate; it is not usually a probability statement about a fixed parameter after observing one interval. Hypothesis tests assess compatibility with a specified null model.~~Statistical inference depends on sampling, independence, model assumptions and multiple testing. Financial time series often violate simple independent-observation assumptions.|Point estimate: One estimated parameter value.;;Confidence interval: Range generated by an uncertainty procedure.;;Hypothesis test: Evidence assessment against a stated null.|Assume 100 independent observations have sample mean 8% and known population standard deviation 20%. Use a normal approximation.~~Standard error = 20% / √100 = 2%.;;A 95% interval is approximately 8% ± 1.96 × 2%.;;The interval is about 4.08% to 11.92%.~~Estimation uncertainty is smaller than individual-observation dispersion.~~Dependence, unknown variance or an unrepresentative sample require revised methods; this simplified interval does not automatically apply to any return dataset.|Define the population and sample.;;Check inference assumptions.;;Report uncertainty and multiple-testing choices.
T02.12|Regression estimates associations between an outcome and explanatory variables. A coefficient describes the fitted change associated with one variable under the model's conditioning assumptions. Statistical significance assesses sampling evidence; economic significance asks whether the magnitude matters after costs and risks.~~Omitted variables, reverse causality, nonstationarity and selection can make coefficients misleading. A low p-value does not prove causation, practical usefulness or out-of-sample predictive power.|Simple regression: One explanatory variable.;;Multiple regression: Several conditioning variables.;;Statistical versus economic significance: Evidence strength versus meaningful magnitude.|A fitted model associates a 1% market return with a 1.2% stock return, plus a monthly intercept of 0.02%. Assume the intercept is statistically distinguishable from zero.~~Interpret slope 1.2 as modelled market sensitivity.;;Annual simple intercept approximation is 0.24%.;;Compare with 0.50% annual implementation costs before claiming usable alpha.~~A statistically significant intercept can be economically unattractive.~~A rigorous investment test also needs risk adjustment, dependence-aware inference and separate evaluation data.|Inspect residuals and specification.;;Assess magnitude after costs.;;Avoid interpreting observational coefficients as automatic causal effects.
T02.13|Time-series analysis studies ordered observations. Stationarity concerns whether relevant distributional properties remain stable over time. Autocorrelation links observations with their past values; volatility clustering means large moves tend to be followed by large moves, regardless of sign.~~Regressing unrelated trending levels can produce spurious relationships. Differencing, transformations and appropriate dynamic models may help, but they must fit the actual data-generating problem rather than mechanically removing useful long-run information.|Stationary versus nonstationary: Stable versus changing relevant properties.;;Autocorrelation: Dependence across lags.;;Conditional volatility: Risk changing with past information.|A price series rises roughly ₹10 each year, while annual changes fluctuate around ₹10.~~Plot levels and changes separately.;;A stable average change does not imply a stable price level.;;Test whether a forecast model should explain changes, returns or an economically justified long-run relationship.~~The choice of transformation changes the question the model answers.~~Formal tests have limitations; structural breaks and changing market conditions can undermine apparently stable historical patterns.|Plot the ordered data.;;Inspect dependence and breaks.;;Validate with time-ordered evaluation rather than random leakage-prone splits.
T02.14|Optimisation selects the best feasible decision under an objective and constraints. Linear algebra represents multiple assets and their covariances; calculus measures marginal changes and helps locate optima. Financial optimisation can amplify estimation errors when inputs are unstable.~~A mathematically optimal solution is only as useful as its objective, constraints and input estimates. Position limits, liquidity, transaction costs and downside requirements can matter more than a tiny improvement in modelled return.|Linear optimisation: Linear objective and constraints.;;Quadratic optimisation: Curved objectives such as portfolio variance.;;Constrained versus unconstrained: Feasibility restrictions included or omitted.|A factory earns ₹4 per unit of A and ₹3 per unit of B. Each uses one machine hour; capacity is 100 hours and demand for A is at most 60 units.~~Maximise 4A + 3B subject to A + B ≤ 100 and A ≤ 60.;;Allocate 60 hours to A, the higher contribution product.;;Allocate the remaining 40 to B, giving ₹240 + ₹120 = ₹360.~~The demand constraint changes the optimum from all-A production.~~Different resource use, setup costs or uncertain demand require a richer model.|Define the objective.;;List all material constraints.;;Test sensitivity to input estimates.
T02.15|Sensitivity analysis changes one input to show its effect. Scenario analysis changes a coherent set of inputs together. Simulation repeatedly draws uncertain inputs from specified distributions and computes outcomes. Each method answers a different question about uncertainty.~~A simulation is not automatically more realistic than a small scenario table. Poor dependence assumptions or implausible distributions can produce highly precise-looking but misleading output.|Sensitivity: One-input change with others fixed.;;Scenario: Internally consistent combination of changes.;;Simulation: Repeated draws under a probabilistic model.|Profit equals revenue minus cost. Base revenue is ₹100 lakh and cost ₹80.~~A revenue-only sensitivity at ₹90 gives ₹10 profit.;;A recession scenario with revenue ₹90 and cost ₹85 gives ₹5 profit.;;A simulation would repeatedly draw revenue and cost jointly and calculate their difference.~~Single-input sensitivity and a joint stress scenario give different losses.~~A probability distribution of profit requires defensible distributions and dependence; three named scenarios alone do not establish probabilities.|Choose the method for the question.;;Preserve realistic input relationships.;;Document assumptions and report uncertainty.
